    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d604">

                <pb facs="0663" n="653" sameAs="frus:frus1943v01/pg653"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Moscow/10–1843</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Secretary of
                        State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-10-27">October 27,
                        1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>During intermission for tea today, Mr. Molotov was even more sociable and
                    friendly and agreeable in every way, if possible, than <pb facs="0664" n="654"
                        xml:id="pg654"/> before. In emphasizing again further the points calling for
                    collaboration and cooperation between our countries, I strongly urged that
                    communications facilities be quickly increased, adding that people simply would
                    not travel back and forth between our two countries if there is only one scant
                    line around by the far out-of-the-way city of Tehran, and that it would be
                    entirely easy to improve greatly these traveling facilities by a small effort
                    and very little attention. He said he agreed with what I had said about the
                    whole matter.</p>
                <p>I then said that my stay at this Conference had been as interesting and enjoyable
                    as any in all my previous experience, but that I find that I am almost obliged
                    to leave here on Sunday next.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d604-f1"/> I then inquired
                    what he thought about it, and he replied that he thought the business of the
                    Conference could be worked out accordingly. I expressed my appreciation and
                    suggested that a committee should be designated to whip together all the odds
                    and ends remaining on the agenda so as to expedite and complete the work in the
                    meantime. To this he agreed. I suggested Mr. Hackworth as my representative and
                    he suggested Mr. Vyshinski, and I think Mr. Eden suggested Mr. Strang.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
